## Solution

**Notation.** Let $\ln^{(1)} x = \ln x$ and $\ln^{(j)} x = \ln\!\bigl(\ln^{(j-1)} x\bigr)$ for $j \ge 2$. Let $e_k$ denote the value where $\ln^{(k)}(e_k)=1$ (so $e_1=e$, $e_2=e^e$, $e_3=e^{e^e}$, …), ensuring every factor in the denominator is defined and positive on $[e_k,\infty)$.

**Key substitution.** Set
$$u = \ln^{(k)} x.$$

We compute its derivative by the chain rule. Since
$$\ln^{(k)} x = \ln\!\bigl(\ln^{(k-1)} x\bigr),$$
we get, unwinding the recursion,
$$\frac{d}{dx}\ln^{(k)} x = \frac{1}{\ln^{(k-1)} x}\cdot\frac{1}{\ln^{(k-2)} x}\cdots\frac{1}{\ln^{(1)} x}\cdot\frac{1}{x} = \frac{1}{x\,\ln x\,\ln^{(2)} x\cdots\ln^{(k-1)} x}.$$

Hence
$$du = \frac{dx}{x\,\ln x\,\ln^{(2)} x\cdots\ln^{(k-1)} x}.$$

**Rewriting the integrand.** The denominator of the given integrand is
$$x\cdot\ln x\cdot\ln^{(2)} x\cdots\ln^{(k-1)} x\cdot\bigl(\ln^{(k)} x\bigr)^{s},$$
so the integrand splits as
$$\frac{dx}{x\,\ln x\,\ln^{(2)} x\cdots\ln^{(k-1)} x\cdot(\ln^{(k)} x)^{s}} = \frac{1}{(\ln^{(k)} x)^{s}}\cdot\frac{dx}{x\,\ln x\cdots\ln^{(k-1)} x} = \frac{du}{u^{s}}.$$

**Change of limits.**
- When $x = e_k$: $\;u = \ln^{(k)}(e_k) = 1$.
- When $x \to \infty$: $\;u = \ln^{(k)} x \to \infty$ (each iterated logarithm tends to $\infty$).

Therefore
$$\int_{e_k}^{\infty}\frac{dx}{x\,\ln x\,\ln^{(2)} x\cdots(\ln^{(k)} x)^{s}} = \int_{1}^{\infty}\frac{du}{u^{s}}.$$

**Convergence of the transformed integral.** The integral $\int_1^\infty u^{-s}\,du$ is the standard $p$-integral at infinity:
- If $s > 1$: $\displaystyle\int_1^\infty u^{-s}\,du = \frac{u^{1-s}}{1-s}\Big|_1^\infty = \frac{1}{s-1} < \infty$ — **converges**.
- If $s = 1$: $\displaystyle\int_1^\infty \frac{du}{u} = \ln u\Big|_1^\infty = \infty$ — **diverges**.
- If $s < 1$: $\displaystyle\int_1^\infty u^{-s}\,du = \frac{u^{1-s}}{1-s}\Big|_1^\infty = \infty$ (since $1-s>0$) — **diverges**.

**Conclusion.** The original integral converges if and only if

$$\boxed{s > 1}.$$

